Hi Eric,

Thanks for the answers, surprising about the fact that the ds()​ function in R won't allow both adjustment terms and covariates in the same model, as it is the function I used and it worked. Here an example of the code I used and I put adjustment termns and covariate in the same model which did not cause error on R:

hr0 <- ds(CTDS_PLWS_VS_Effort, transect = "point", key ="hr", adjustment = NULL,
          convert_units = conversion, cutpoints = mybreaks, truncation = trunc.list)

hr0cov <- ds(CTDS_PLWS_VS_Effort, transect = "point", formula = ~Habitat, key ="hr", adjustment = NULL,
          convert_units = conversion, cutpoints = mybreaks, truncation = trunc.list)

hr1 <- ds(CTDS_PLWS_VS_Effort, transect = "point", key ="hr", adjustment = "poly",
          nadj = 1, convert_units = conversion, cutpoints = mybreaks, truncation = trunc.list)

hr1cov <- ds(CTDS_PLWS_VS_Effort, transect = "point", formula = ~Habitat, key ="hr", adjustment = "poly",
          nadj = 1, convert_units = conversion, cutpoints = mybreaks, truncation = trunc.list)

After removing all the models that had algorithm problems, I end up with the following candidate models:
the uniform key with 1, and 2 cosine adjustments

the half normal key with 0, 1 and 2 cosine adjustments

the half normal key with no cosine adjustments + Covariate Habitat

the hazard rate key with 0 and 1 simple polynomial adjustments

the hazard rate key with no adjustments + Covariate Habitat

Here the results I obtain after using the two-step process.

QAIC values for half normal key models.
                df       QAIC
hn0          1    85.06153
hn1          2    85.95132
hn0cov    2    86.66111

QAIC values for uniform key models.
            df    QAIC
uni1    1    76.29592
uni2    2    77.72735
uni3    3    78.40180

QAIC values for hazard rate key models.
               df        QAIC
hr0          2    128.9022
hr0cov    3    130.1884
hr1          3    130.9022

>From this first pass of model selection based on QAIC values, we find the preferable model with the hazard rate and half-normal key function is one without adjustment terms. The model with the uniform key function preferred by QAIC has a single adjustment term. The second step of model selection ranks the models by their c^ values.

modnames                                                                       chats
hazard-rate key function                                                 5.16
half-normal key function                                                12.42
uniform key function with cosine(1) adjustments    12.78

For this data set, the model chosen by the algorithm that adjusts for overdispersion is the hazard rate key without adjustments. it is different from the model chosen by conventional model selection, which is hazard rate key without adjustments + Cov habitat. Is that correct ? Why is it that when I do this two-step process, the model chosen at the end is the one without covariate ?

Thank you again for your help.

Best regards,

Emeline

Le lundi 13 février 2023 à 15:37:16 UTC+7, Eric Rexstad a écrit :

Emeline

Before addressing your model selection question, some questions arise from looking at the table you provided.

My attention is drawn to the column of standard errors for P_a. This column tells me something about whether the parameter estimation algorithm had difficulty. For each of the models that had the habitat covariate plus adjustment terms, the standard error is very large, suggesting a problem with the estimation algorithm. You should examine the output for each model more closely, but I would suggest inference should not be made from those models, namely
HZ with simple
HN with cos 2,3 (note P_a estimate to be 0 for this model)
HN with cos 2
even HN cos 2,3 without the habitat covariate has an extremely large se(P_a). As I say, I wouldn't make inference from any of these models, regardless of what model selection tells me.  The ds()​ function in R won't allow both adjustment terms and covariates in the same model, so not sure how some of those models came to appear in your table.

After removing all the models that had algorithm problems, the QAIC model selection process might be more straightforward. Follow the steps shown in the duiker example analysis on our examples page

https://examples.distancesampling.org/Distance-cameratraps/camera-distill.html#model-selection-adjustments-from-overdispersion
